vsnprintf must report truncation the way ISO callers read it.

    snprintf(buf, 4, "hello")
        glibc:   returns 5   -- what it WOULD have written
        M2libc:  returns 3   -- what it DID write

C says the return is the length the call would have produced, ignoring the
limit. Every grow-and-retry caller is written against that:

    len = vsnprintf(buf, size, fmt, ap);
    if (len >= size) { grow to len + 1; retry; }        tccpp.c cstr_vprintf

With the written count returned, len is never >= size, so the caller believes
every string fit and takes a truncated message as complete.

WHY IT SURVIVED 0007. That patch rewrote this function's conversion parsing and
kept the existing return deliberately -- its header says "Resume contract
unchanged" -- because M2libc's own vfprintf uses this function as a CHUNKING
engine and accumulated the return as the size of each chunk. Two callers, two
contracts, and the in-tree one was the only one checked. The 51 checks added
with 0007 compare formatted OUTPUT against glibc and not one of them reads the
return value on truncation, which is exactly why this got through.

WHAT CHANGES. The two truncation exits return n -- at or above the limit, which
is what "truncated" has to look like -- and vfprintf accumulates strlen of the
buffer it is about to write rather than the return value, which is what it
actually wanted all along. The resume protocol via __vsnprintf_string_offset
and __vsnprintf_ap is untouched, so the chunking still works.

NOT FULLY ISO. A truncated call returns n rather than the exact would-be
length: this function stops at the truncation point by design so the caller can
resume, and computing the true total would mean parsing the rest of the format
twice. Grow-and-retry callers are correct because they only compare against the
limit; a caller doing `p += snprintf(...)` past the end would not be. That is
recorded here rather than left to be discovered.

MEASURED: the existing 51 checks still pass, and the new truncation checks in
test-vsnprintf.sh fail before this patch and pass after.


CORRECTION. The first cut of this patch had vfprintf accumulate
strlen(printf_buf). strlen appears nowhere else in stdio.c, so in any build
that compiles this file alone the symbol is undefined -- 40 corpus rows went
from passing to "WILL NOT COMPILE: stdio.c:967:str". It passed my testing
because I built it with gcc, where strlen comes from the host's <string.h>,
and never through micro-c.

vsnprintf already knows how many bytes it wrote, so it exports the count as
__vsnprintf_written beside the resume state, and vfprintf reads that. No new
symbol, and nothing recomputed.

--- a/stdio.c
+++ b/stdio.c
@@ -512,6 +512,13 @@ char* __integer_to_string(int value)
 
 int __vsnprintf_string_offset;
 va_list __vsnprintf_ap;
+/* BYTES ACTUALLY WRITTEN by the last vsnprintf call, for vfprintf's chunking
+ * loop. The RETURN value reports truncation the way ISO callers read it and is
+ * therefore no longer the size of the chunk; this is. Exported rather than
+ * recomputed because the function already knows it, and because the obvious
+ * alternative -- strlen of the buffer -- adds a symbol stdio.c does not
+ * otherwise use, which is exactly what broke 40 corpus rows. */
+int __vsnprintf_written;
 /* One line since M2-Mesoplanet doesn't support multi line macros */
 #define INLINE_STRSCPY str_i = 0; while(str[str_i] != '\0' && output < n - 1) { s[output++] = str[str_i++]; }
 /* A CONVERSION SPECIFICATION IS NOT JUST ITS LAST LETTER.
@@ -610,8 +617,14 @@ int vsnprintf(char* s, size_t n, const char* format, va_list arg)
 			{
 				__vsnprintf_string_offset = i;
 				__vsnprintf_ap = arg;
+				__vsnprintf_written = output;
 				s[output] = '\0';
-				return output;
+				/* TRUNCATED: SAY SO THE WAY ISO CALLERS READ IT. See the note
+				 * above vfprintf. Returning the bytes WRITTEN makes
+				 *     len = vsnprintf(buf, size, ...);
+				 *     if (len >= size) { grow; retry; }
+				 * believe every string fit. */
+				return n;
 			}
 			s[output] = format[i];
 			output = output + 1;
@@ -865,8 +878,10 @@ int vsnprintf(char* s, size_t n, const char* format, va_list arg)
 		{
 			__vsnprintf_string_offset = spec_start;
 			__vsnprintf_ap = ap_save;
+			__vsnprintf_written = output;
 			s[output] = '\0';
-			return output;
+			/* TRUNCATED -- same reason as the site above. */
+			return n;
 		}
 
 		if(!flag_minus && !flag_zero)
@@ -926,6 +941,7 @@ int vsnprintf(char* s, size_t n, const char* format, va_list arg)
 
 	__vsnprintf_string_offset = i;
 	__vsnprintf_ap = arg;
+	__vsnprintf_written = output;
 	if(format[i] == '\0') __vsnprintf_string_offset = 0;
 
 	if(output < n)
@@ -952,7 +968,13 @@ int vfprintf(FILE* stream, char* format, va_list arg)
 
 	do
 	{
-		output += vsnprintf(printf_buf, PRINTF_BUFFER_SIZE, format, ap);
+		/* WHAT WAS WRITTEN, NOT WHAT WAS RETURNED.
+		 *
+		 * vsnprintf's return now reports TRUNCATION for ISO callers, so it is
+		 * no longer the size of this chunk. This loop wants the bytes it is
+		 * about to hand to fputs, which is exactly strlen of the buffer. */
+		vsnprintf(printf_buf, PRINTF_BUFFER_SIZE, format, ap);
+		output += __vsnprintf_written;
 		format += __vsnprintf_string_offset;
 		ap = __vsnprintf_ap;
 		fputs(printf_buf, stream);
